  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "475f8fcf-60c9-45e9-b7a1-ffd1deb82b8f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "475f8fcf-60c9-45e9-b7a1-ffd1deb82b8f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "80a5c8ed-b21a-4ed5-bbd5-42508d31e12d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e71b2ee9-70a0-47b4-a8b5-fa1eeb77ffa5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Activate students' real-world experiences with apps and build a shared list that will be used to identify patterns in how different apps function in a browser versus on a phone and begin forming an informal definition of a web app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students list two or three apps they use every day, then turn to a partner to share why one is their favorite. Call on a few volunteers to share with the class and record their responses on the board to reference in the next slide.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to include a mix of app types, such as tools, games, or utilities, not just social media apps. This creates a more diverse list for later discussion."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e71b2ee9-70a0-47b4-a8b5-fa1eeb77ffa5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "18d0d413-aff2-4af4-8529-48dc7830bc5c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f2496f9-bf13-4034-92dc-f858a373317a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f2496f9-bf13-4034-92dc-f858a373317a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "18d0d413-aff2-4af4-8529-48dc7830bc5c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eeb6c29f-80cb-4024-aa21-fc6e8f91d428",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What do you notice about how they look or work in a browser?*\n- *What makes them web apps instead of just apps you install on your phone?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that web apps are accessed through a browser rather than installed on a device and begin to notice patterns such as needing internet access, working across devices, and being updated automatically. The key takeaway is that web apps live online and can be used anywhere, which connects to how design choices affect accessibility and usability.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle, display or describe examples (e.g., YouTube, Google Docs) and prompt with: \"Can you use it without downloading anything?\" or \"What happens when you open it on a computer?\" to lead them toward the idea that web apps run inside browsers."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eeb6c29f-80cb-4024-aa21-fc6e8f91d428",
        "lesson_activity.key": "18d0d413-aff2-4af4-8529-48dc7830bc5c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c4010b36-ca19-4bfa-b951-6e2b9dc19e32",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c4010b36-ca19-4bfa-b951-6e2b9dc19e32",
        "lesson_activity.key": "18d0d413-aff2-4af4-8529-48dc7830bc5c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9dc85caf-4520-4163-97db-d8d5b178906a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9dc85caf-4520-4163-97db-d8d5b178906a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "18d0d413-aff2-4af4-8529-48dc7830bc5c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7a1f11c5-1285-43f8-8618-f91b5fce4305",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Guide students to recognize what makes a web app distinct from an installed app through hands-on observation, discussion, and AI-supported exploration of how HTML and CSS shape web experiences.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the apps on the slide. Ask students to consider how they use or access them: Which open in a browser, and which only work when downloaded? Have a few volunteers share examples.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Basics of Web Apps",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students mention both versions (e.g., \"Spotify can do both\"), use that to emphasize overlap—some apps exist as both installed and web versions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7a1f11c5-1285-43f8-8618-f91b5fce4305",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "121297da-1641-4b62-a167-a834d75d51c7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "121297da-1641-4b62-a167-a834d75d51c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "193aed99-3e22-40f6-b405-55409699b06f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Which of these web apps let you do things instead of just look at or read content?*\n- *What kinds of actions do you take in those web apps?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students deepen their understanding of what makes web apps unique by comparing how different apps are accessed and used. They recognize that while some apps are installed and device-specific, others run in a browser and let users interact—create, post, comment, or edit—directly online. Through this comparison, students identify that web apps allow users to interact and create, not just view content. They begin connecting this interactivity to design and coding concepts, setting up how HTML and CSS combine to make web apps dynamic.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students give generic answers (\"You can post on all of them\"), ask: \"How does the app show that it's responding to you?\" or  \"What makes that interaction possible?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "193aed99-3e22-40f6-b405-55409699b06f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7f86a9e-ced9-4a74-bb10-9f22e4a55b8f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7f86a9e-ced9-4a74-bb10-9f22e4a55b8f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca859285-7205-4077-ba47-fdfc678dd47b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Welcome to Web Apps activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students they'll use the guide to record what they notice about how web pages are built as they explore examples.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define HTML and CSS. Explain that HTML provides the structure and content of a page, while CSS adds the style and visual design. Direct students to look closely at the two versions of the page on the slide. Have them record in their activity guide what they think HTML is controlling and what CSS is controlling based on what they notice in each image.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to share a few observations before you confirm or elaborate. Use simple analogies to keep it intuitive:\n- HTML is the skeleton or blueprint that shapes the page.\n- CSS is the paint, layout, and personality that make it visually appealing.\n\nRemind students that they don't need to know any code yet—just focus on what seems to belong to structure versus style."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca859285-7205-4077-ba47-fdfc678dd47b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df9c2a06-d361-4c87-8e65-5555fdd44ba5",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df9c2a06-d361-4c87-8e65-5555fdd44ba5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "87899bfa-8a7d-4c47-99dc-04a9e2157b47",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students see that HTML and CSS each serve different roles, and that both are needed to make a webpage functional and visually appealing."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "87899bfa-8a7d-4c47-99dc-04a9e2157b47",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c5b8f54-3643-438e-9661-1a432cc41f54",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you'll test what happens when a web page has only HTML or only CSS. This will help you see how each one contributes to the final page design.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c5b8f54-3643-438e-9661-1a432cc41f54",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "28e8f7be-de39-4726-8f2e-123893a82ab4",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 and 2.\n- Level 1: Students prompt AI to create a webpage with only HTML.\n- Level 2: Students prompt AI to create a webpage with only CSS.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Remind students to add any new observations to their activity guide as they notice what each language controls or lacks. Check that students are noticing the limitations at each level. Ask questions like: \"What’s missing from this page when it’s only HTML?\" \"What’s missing when it’s only CSS?\"",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Generating Web Pages",
        "progression_name": "Producing HTML and CSS",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students may think CSS can create an entire webpage by itself. Reinforce that CSS depends on HTML's structure—CSS tells the browser how to display content, but HTML provides what to display."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "28e8f7be-de39-4726-8f2e-123893a82ab4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bae7de0b-f904-4151-9838-46158da14145",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bae7de0b-f904-4151-9838-46158da14145",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "81051ceb-97dc-4e7a-bdab-7f9a5fb27a4c",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What did you notice about each page’s look and feel?*\n- *Which human decisions made one easier to use?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that both HTML and CSS contribute to a webpage's usability and overall experience—HTML defines the structure and content, while CSS adds design and visual hierarchy. They also begin connecting these technical roles to human-centered design decisions, understanding that choices like color, spacing, and readability all affect how users experience a page.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle to articulate the differences, project the HTML-only and CSS-only outputs side by side and ask: \"What’s missing here?\" \"Which one shows content? Which one shows style?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "81051ceb-97dc-4e7a-bdab-7f9a5fb27a4c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "20ecff69-5371-465a-98b2-2e39cebd009a",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "20ecff69-5371-465a-98b2-2e39cebd009a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0f88da61-05d2-4ff2-99c4-ca0a54614a8a",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How do you think the AI knew what to write?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students begin to wonder about how AI systems produce responses and form initial hypotheses about where the AI's \"knowledge\" comes from. The goal is to surface students' prior ideas. Some may assume the AI is programmed with all the answers, while others may guess it has learned from examples. This primes curiosity for the next slide, where they'll explore how AI actually learns from patterns in data.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Validate all ideas shared, emphasizing that this is about thinking like investigators, not knowing the right answer. If discussion stalls, prompt with:\n- Do you think someone taught it what to write?\n- Does it have rules built in, or did it learn from examples?\n- How is that similar or different from how you learn to code or design?\n\nLet students' reasoning stay open-ended. You'll connect it to how AI really works on the following slide."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0f88da61-05d2-4ff2-99c4-ca0a54614a8a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "607c1bce-4316-4037-9f10-3600f9ec714e",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "607c1bce-4316-4037-9f10-3600f9ec714e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "84a9c85b-6879-4768-9dd0-b42d2a530cc1",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We just guessed how the AI might have known what to write for us. This short video will help explain how AI tools actually learn to make predictions and generate things like HTML and CSS.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "84a9c85b-6879-4768-9dd0-b42d2a530cc1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf32af56-ef90-4362-b61a-9938a55ef789",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"How AI Works - Machine Learning\" video.\n\n**Questions to consider with the video:** \n\n- What kind of data do you think our AI tool was trained on to make your HTML and CSS code?\n- How might that training data affect what the AI creates?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students begin to understand that AI tools learn by recognizing patterns in large sets of existing examples—in this case, examples of HTML and CSS code written by humans. They realize that the AI doesn't \"think\" or \"know\" how to code; instead, it generates code by predicting what comes next based on patterns it has seen before. Students also connect this idea to the earlier activity: just as CSS depends on HTML for structure, the AI depends on human-created data for guidance and context.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point out that even though the AI can generate both structure (HTML) and style (CSS), the content and purpose still come from human decisions. This reinforces that AI is a tool that extends human creativity, not a replacement for it."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf32af56-ef90-4362-b61a-9938a55ef789",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d2f620ad-2eca-4c58-8097-103e3ea936ec",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d2f620ad-2eca-4c58-8097-103e3ea936ec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7c2ca3c3-f5ad-43a8-806f-2ae5ee97faa4",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 3 through 7.\n- Level 3: Students will read the official definition for HTML.\n- Level 4: Students will add their own notes or examples that help them remember what HTML does.\n- Level 5: Students will read the official definition for CSS.\n- Level 6: Students will add their own notes or examples that help them remember what CSS controls.\n- Level 7: Students answer a Check for Understanding question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for evidence that they understand the difference between structure/content (HTML) and style/appearance (CSS). Encourage students to use their own language and examples when adding context.",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: HTML"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7c2ca3c3-f5ad-43a8-806f-2ae5ee97faa4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2cf00721-a2ad-437f-a8c5-00ecc8d0224d",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: CSS"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2cf00721-a2ad-437f-a8c5-00ecc8d0224d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "06723e7d-32b7-404f-82c6-a5f8d13e498d",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "- For the Vocabulary levels, if students restate definitions word-for-word, prompt them to personalize their responses: \"What's an example from the app you just explored that shows HTML's role?”\"or \"How could you explain CSS to a friend using an analogy?\"\n- For the Check for Understanding level, if students give vague responses to the Check for Understanding questions (\"HTML is important\" / \"CSS makes it look better\"), push them to be specific with sentence frames like: \"HTML contributes by ___.\" or \"CSS contributes by ___.\""
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student responses to gauge whether they can clearly explain the complementary roles of HTML and CSS and how both are essential to building usable, visually appealing web pages."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "06723e7d-32b7-404f-82c6-a5f8d13e498d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84de7442-0a1c-412b-ba77-818f104eddcc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7c832e35-ddc1-4c6e-b661-b6ce390aa75c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How do web apps shape our daily lives?*\n- *Who builds these apps?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Student should share their responses to Level 7 Check for Understanding question which has them connect today's lesson to their own experiences with technology. They should recognize that web apps shape how people communicate, learn, and create by being accessible on any device and designed for interaction. Students also acknowledge that web apps are built by teams of humans—designers, developers, and problem-solvers—who make decisions about how apps look, feel, and function. This reinforces that web apps are human-built systems that combine both technical structure and human-centered design choices that shape user experience.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As the discussion wraps up, make sure to reinforce the connection between today’s technical and design ideas. Emphasize:\n- HTML is the structure—it controls what the page does.\n- CSS is the style—it controls how the page feels.\n- Together, they shape our experience with web apps.\n\nUse this as a final talking point to help students see how both code and human design decisions contribute to the apps they use every day."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7c832e35-ddc1-4c6e-b661-b6ce390aa75c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7ee4e09e-fe5b-4d6c-8916-4f4f3ca1962d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7fe37fd0-6931-4cd4-983a-65d455844e02",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7fe37fd0-6931-4cd4-983a-65d455844e02",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7ee4e09e-fe5b-4d6c-8916-4f4f3ca1962d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "186329e4-e93d-482a-960b-a8fb98f144f9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "186329e4-e93d-482a-960b-a8fb98f144f9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7ee4e09e-fe5b-4d6c-8916-4f4f3ca1962d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e5e5dbc-21a7-4b5c-b30a-4328515971ec",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 8 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e5e5dbc-21a7-4b5c-b30a-4328515971ec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7ee4e09e-fe5b-4d6c-8916-4f4f3ca1962d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "876844a0-32d9-4ca7-831f-d8f365a0ed5f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Engage students with a visual, real-world example that makes usability differences obvious. Help them recognize that design evolves to meet user needs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show two versions of the same website from different years (e.g., Amazon in 2000 and 2025)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "876844a0-32d9-4ca7-831f-d8f365a0ed5f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "abcee514-b03e-49bd-a11a-7e9a97464c96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08f530bc-e68a-43cd-915f-976c754bc89d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let's take a look at how web design has changed over time. Here's what Amazon.com looked like in 2000 and 2025. Take a moment to look closely at the differences in design.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08f530bc-e68a-43cd-915f-976c754bc89d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "abcee514-b03e-49bd-a11a-7e9a97464c96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb1bac17-ea06-40bc-9cbc-2902a7245b74",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which version would you rather use and why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Listen for students noticing specific usability improvements, not just visual ones. Look for comments that highlight how modern websites feel easier to navigate, look cleaner and less cluttered, and are more accessible or efficient. Students might mention clearer menus, readable text, or AI-driven personalization that helps them find what they need faster.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students focus on looks, prompt them to think about usability. Ask, \"What makes it easier to find what you need?\" or \"How might this help different users?\" Highlight features like search bars or cleaner layouts as signs of human-centered, accessible design. You can also note that modern sites adapt to users."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb1bac17-ea06-40bc-9cbc-2902a7245b74",
        "lesson_activity.key": "abcee514-b03e-49bd-a11a-7e9a97464c96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5e2db51c-fbad-44ae-8c44-1e2842165b1c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5e2db51c-fbad-44ae-8c44-1e2842165b1c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "abcee514-b03e-49bd-a11a-7e9a97464c96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c7c59845-165b-40b1-8a0a-e1cd18d74db4",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that we've compared the old and new Amazon sites, let's take a closer look at the newer one. It's a great example of how modern websites are designed around people's everyday needs — clearer visuals, faster access, and more personal experiences.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c7c59845-165b-40b1-8a0a-e1cd18d74db4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "abcee514-b03e-49bd-a11a-7e9a97464c96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c83748b-ce80-4bd2-b4d4-0cdbdd48e32d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Keep the 2025 Amazon site on screen. Ask students to look closely and describe what makes it feel easier or more useful for real people today.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Why do you think there are more pictures now?*\n- *What makes it easier to find or buy things?*\n- *How might the site change based on who's using it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should note how the site reflects people's habits and needs, such as being able to see items before buying, simple layouts that make shopping quicker, and personalized suggestions that save time. These observations help students see that design is becoming more human-centered — built around what people need and do.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students mention AI features (like \"it knows what I want\"), connect it lightly: \"That's right, even AI can be part of human-centered design when it helps people find what they need.\" You can also mention that modern sites adapt to different devices and users — a sign of human-centered design"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c83748b-ce80-4bd2-b4d4-0cdbdd48e32d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "abcee514-b03e-49bd-a11a-7e9a97464c96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c4eebec-3fc6-4f96-94df-7b3f26933f02",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c4eebec-3fc6-4f96-94df-7b3f26933f02",
        "lesson_activity.key": "abcee514-b03e-49bd-a11a-7e9a97464c96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fcc1c320-4ed4-47ce-b6e7-c499aa5b1dc4",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fcc1c320-4ed4-47ce-b6e7-c499aa5b1dc4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "abcee514-b03e-49bd-a11a-7e9a97464c96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c34dddf7-d06b-4aa9-9da4-619fe0138ede",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Introduce students to Human-Centered Design (HCD) as the idea that good design starts with understanding people's needs, and show how this connects to User Experience (UX) Design—the process of putting that idea into practice. Students learn that HCD is the mindset (empathy and understanding), while UX design is the work designers do to make technology easy, clear, and enjoyable for users.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define human-centered design.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Intro to Human-Centered and User Experience Design"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c34dddf7-d06b-4aa9-9da4-619fe0138ede",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a31a300c-6775-42f0-aa13-955ca72a0fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "51bc46fc-d9cc-4e7d-ad29-cede7e7739e8",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Think about what we just saw on the modern Amazon site: it's full of visuals, easy to navigate, and even adjusts to your interests. Those choices came from understanding what people need and how they actually shop. That's what it means to design with the user in mind.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "51bc46fc-d9cc-4e7d-ad29-cede7e7739e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a31a300c-6775-42f0-aa13-955ca72a0fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "90c93086-1d50-43dc-b78e-cf7ff304ce68",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the Amazon screenshot with callouts showing examples of human-centered design choices (e.g., large images, clear categories, personalized recommendations). Walk students through each design decision one at a time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Do you think these choices help everyone equally? Why or why not?*\n- *Who might find this design confusing or less helpful?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize how that even designs meant to help users can still work better for some people than others. Encourage them to consider factors like ability, access, identity, or personal preferences. Encourage students to think about whose experiences are missing and why fairness and accessibility matter in design. Students might note that:\n- People who can't see well might struggle with image-heavy layouts.\n- Recommendations might not show the same options to everyone.\n- Some users might not see their style or interests represented."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "90c93086-1d50-43dc-b78e-cf7ff304ce68",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a31a300c-6775-42f0-aa13-955ca72a0fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3fe1c461-0155-480f-83ec-d22afffdbc05",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the clip from the \"What is UX Design?\" video.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What skills do you think a UX designer needs to make sure technology works for people?*\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that UX designers use a mix of empathy, observation, creativity, and problem-solving to make technology fit users' needs. Students might say: listening, testing, creativity, empathy, noticing details.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 6.\n- Level 1: Students read the definition for user experience.\n- Level 2: Students write the definition in their own words and/or include examples.\n- Level 3: Students read the definition for user profile.\n- Level 4: Students write the definition in their own words and/or include examples.\n- Level 5: Students read the definition for human-centered design.\n- Level 6: Students write the definition in their own words and/or include examples.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Encourage students to use their own language and examples when adding context.",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: User Experience"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3fe1c461-0155-480f-83ec-d22afffdbc05",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a31a300c-6775-42f0-aa13-955ca72a0fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92dc30a1-3213-4506-afb6-16154c4520df",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: User Profile"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92dc30a1-3213-4506-afb6-16154c4520df",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a31a300c-6775-42f0-aa13-955ca72a0fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9dd3034b-7761-48ff-a34a-f99716836cba",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Human-Centered Design"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9dd3034b-7761-48ff-a34a-f99716836cba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a31a300c-6775-42f0-aa13-955ca72a0fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "83ae0d18-c279-43c4-b263-3cfc65a65d13",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students practice empathy by seeing apps through the eyes of different users. Reinforce that usability depends on the context and the person, not just the design itself. Provide a structured, hands-on critique activity that makes user-centered design concrete.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "See It Like a User"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "83ae0d18-c279-43c4-b263-3cfc65a65d13",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a31a300c-6775-42f0-aa13-955ca72a0fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "466505b5-bf0e-429b-b23b-8e8f88e65132",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you'll step into the role of a UX designer. You'll look at your app through a user's eyes and think about how design affects different people.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "466505b5-bf0e-429b-b23b-8e8f88e65132",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a31a300c-6775-42f0-aa13-955ca72a0fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "04474a1b-e812-4a45-a7cd-3257f1173269",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the three UX Designer tasks: Observe, Evaluate, Suggest. Explain that these are the same steps real designers use to make technology more human-centered.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in groups of three.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of The User's Experience activity guide and several markers or colored pencils."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "04474a1b-e812-4a45-a7cd-3257f1173269",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a31a300c-6775-42f0-aa13-955ca72a0fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d15f84b-9c4f-49f4-9443-69e6ff3509a1",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Each person in your group will represent one of these user profiles: Jordan, Mark, or Sam. Students should read through their assigned user profile in their activity guide and think about their needs and how they might use an app.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d15f84b-9c4f-49f4-9443-69e6ff3509a1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a31a300c-6775-42f0-aa13-955ca72a0fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5ae5673-d4b1-4b4a-8cb4-4fdf9ca787c7",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Each person takes one user profile (Jordan, Mark, or Sam). Students review the app and annotate it using the Usability Checklist, focusing on how well it meets their user's needs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct groups to discussion their ratings and annotations and identify any patterns across the different user profiles.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Students should begin to recognize that usability is not absolute but depends on the user's context and needs. Encourage students to share specific examples from their critiques. Listen for students connecting specific app features (or lack thereof) to the needs of their user profiles.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 7.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students to make specific, user-centered suggestions for improvement. Probe students who are struggling to connect their critique to a concrete change. Ask: \"Why would that specific change be important for Jordan/Mark/Sam?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to connect their suggested change to the idea of empathy and how would this change make life easier for their user."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "This prompt can serve as a formative assessment. Collect student responses to gauge their understanding of applying user-centered design principles."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5ae5673-d4b1-4b4a-8cb4-4fdf9ca787c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a31a300c-6775-42f0-aa13-955ca72a0fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e23aac59-d245-4df9-bf34-35942ffdf08e",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e23aac59-d245-4df9-bf34-35942ffdf08e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a31a300c-6775-42f0-aa13-955ca72a0fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af98e859-6abb-4a23-b60b-c078a22e2b4f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Consolidate the lesson by naming Human-Centered Design explicitly. Transition students from activity-based observations to formal vocabulary and concepts. Provide an early writing artifact that demonstrates growth in HCD understanding."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af98e859-6abb-4a23-b60b-c078a22e2b4f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4430529a-a8ac-4f2d-9148-c240ad30f9cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1af654b9-7c29-4afc-80ab-a673e326f8da",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We've spent today looking at how different people use apps and websites. We've seen how design choices can make things easy or frustrating. This whole process is part of something called Human-Centered Design, or HCD. It means that good design always starts with empathy – truly understanding and caring about the users.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1af654b9-7c29-4afc-80ab-a673e326f8da",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4430529a-a8ac-4f2d-9148-c240ad30f9cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7ee7b399-4d45-4c1c-a9f4-1caf674a430d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to share what they wrote for the Level 7 prompt: \"If I designed with [user profile], I'd change ___.\" Encourage them to refer back to the reasons they noted with their color ratings. Students will articulate specific design changes based on the needs of their user profile, demonstrating an understanding of user-centered design.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7ee7b399-4d45-4c1c-a9f4-1caf674a430d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4430529a-a8ac-4f2d-9148-c240ad30f9cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fda13a81-2db7-4a05-912c-8105f30a02be",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 8 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fda13a81-2db7-4a05-912c-8105f30a02be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4430529a-a8ac-4f2d-9148-c240ad30f9cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef3ff3a6-a92f-47fb-8d90-1fc875422f14",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize that AI can generate web layouts quickly but still requires human oversight to ensure quality, accuracy, and usability.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show video demonstrating AI building an app.\n\n**Questions to consider with the video:** \n- What do you notice about what the AI did well versus what a human might still need to check?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify that while AI speeds up development, human developers remain responsible for checking the work, refining outputs, and making design decisions. This sets the stage for understanding iteration and accountability in AI-assisted development.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Would you trust or edit this output if you were the developer? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students consider the reliability of AI-generated code and the importance of human oversight. Emphasize that reviewing, testing, and refining AI code is part of a developer's job—AI can speed up creation, but humans remain responsible for ensuring quality, accuracy, and safety. This discussion should reinforce ethical awareness and human accountability in AI-assisted development.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students say they'd \"just trust it,\" prompt them to think critically by asking: \"What could go wrong if the AI made a small mistake?\" or \"Who's responsible if something doesn't work or breaks a site?\"\nThis helps students articulate that humans—not AI—own the final product."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef3ff3a6-a92f-47fb-8d90-1fc875422f14",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e9d28ccd-23a1-46d7-8324-0d23eb888b4e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f63892cb-cf3a-4ac3-9cfe-5fcfd7558eb5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f63892cb-cf3a-4ac3-9cfe-5fcfd7558eb5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e9d28ccd-23a1-46d7-8324-0d23eb888b4e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "06a10236-e5f8-492c-8cc2-600bca9efc3b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How might this make the developer's job easier?*\n- *Where could it make the job harder?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students consider how AI can change the development process—making some parts faster or simpler while introducing new challenges. They recognize that AI can boost productivity but also misinterpret instructions or produce flawed code, requiring human review and refinement. This discussion should aim to build nuance around the idea of AI as a helpful but fallible partner.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "06a10236-e5f8-492c-8cc2-600bca9efc3b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e9d28ccd-23a1-46d7-8324-0d23eb888b4e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d956eb0-d7d3-4992-9234-52bee7b6a4c9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students understand that AI generates code by predicting patterns from examples of real web pages—not by reasoning or understanding. This idea sets the foundation for why precise prompting and iteration are important.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use the slide to explain to students that before they start using AI to write code, think about how it actually knows what to write. Remind students that AI doesn't really ‘understand' code the way a developer does — it predicts patterns it has seen in examples of real web pages.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why might AI sometimes misunderstand what we mean?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students hypothesize possible causes of AI misunderstanding prompts—such as unclear wording, missing details, conflicting instructions, or biases in training data. The goal isn't for them to know exactly how models work, but to start reasoning about why clarity and iteration are essential in coding with AI.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Exploring Prompting",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Validate all ideas. Highlight that these misunderstandings are normal and exactly why iteration matters: developers test, adjust, and refine prompts to guide AI toward what they want.\nIf discussion stalls, prompt with:\n- Have you ever written a message and someone misunderstood you? What caused that?\n- How could that happen between a person and an AI?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d956eb0-d7d3-4992-9234-52bee7b6a4c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2775f1d4-33de-43d3-847e-c715a3082fc3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2150d41e-30a8-4bf4-927b-e38e9f01e3d1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2150d41e-30a8-4bf4-927b-e38e9f01e3d1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2775f1d4-33de-43d3-847e-c715a3082fc3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "96289fd0-9c66-4454-95ad-b2c8a9f7dd2c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly review the three strategies with students as an introduction to prompting. Explain that they'll get more detailed guidance and examples in Level 1's Prompting & Versioning panel, which they can revisit whenever they need support.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, ask students a follow-up question: \"Why might being specific and organized help the AI understand you better?\" Let a few quick ideas surface before transitioning to Level 1."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "96289fd0-9c66-4454-95ad-b2c8a9f7dd2c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2775f1d4-33de-43d3-847e-c715a3082fc3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1828ae42-8ce0-41c9-abf1-3c77a2169719",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1828ae42-8ce0-41c9-abf1-3c77a2169719",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2775f1d4-33de-43d3-847e-c715a3082fc3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b47b54f8-6fd9-4045-8ec2-48e2e981613b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Web Page Features activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 7.\n- Level 1: Students review the prompting strategies and learn about version control.\n- Level 2: Students explore the example webpage and identify its main features on their Web Page Features activity guide\n- Level 3: Students try prompting the AI to recreate one feature from Level 2 and prompt the AI to revise as needed.\n- Level 4: Students read the vocabulary definition for prompt engineering.\n- Level 5: Students add their own explanation or example of prompt engineering to the vocab free response.\n- Level 6: Students read the vocabulary definition for versioning.\n- Level 7: Students add their own explanation or example of versioning to the vocab free response.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, check that they're using details from their prompts (not just \"make a nav bar\"). Ask students questions like, \"What changed after you refined your prompt?\" or \"Why did you save that version?\" Make sure to reinforce that iteration—trying, refining, and saving—is how developers manage complex builds."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b47b54f8-6fd9-4045-8ec2-48e2e981613b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2775f1d4-33de-43d3-847e-c715a3082fc3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e979854f-aeb9-48a7-82a2-545d17ef6651",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Web Page Features"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e979854f-aeb9-48a7-82a2-545d17ef6651",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2775f1d4-33de-43d3-847e-c715a3082fc3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "11625c10-8410-4c66-94e9-7285f7f476e5",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Prompt Engineering"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "11625c10-8410-4c66-94e9-7285f7f476e5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2775f1d4-33de-43d3-847e-c715a3082fc3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f10d01f9-2b1c-4994-b375-8efac829da78",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Versioning",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that refining prompts is normal and expected. Encourage them to focus on clear communication—naming elements, structure, and purpose. Connect this to real developer practice: \"Developers don't just write perfect code once. They iterate, version, and improve—just like you're doing here.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f10d01f9-2b1c-4994-b375-8efac829da78",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2775f1d4-33de-43d3-847e-c715a3082fc3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7771ab9-d592-4875-b0b8-f0414e2fc1ce",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7771ab9-d592-4875-b0b8-f0414e2fc1ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2775f1d4-33de-43d3-847e-c715a3082fc3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e433a6a5-6cc0-4a57-909b-06a7131d82ea",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Why do developers iterate?*\n- *What does it say about our role when the AI doesn't get it right the first time?*\n- *How do you think real developers use AI like this?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect iteration to real development practice. They recognize that iteration isn't just rework—it's how developers refine, debug, and improve both their own instructions and AI outputs. Students also understand that AI speeds up creation but still relies on human judgment, creativity, and accountability for quality results.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students focus only on AI's mistakes, reframe the conversation: Iteration is how humans guide the AI toward what we actually want. What does that tell us about the developer's role? After students respond, connect explicitly to industry practice: AI can generate layouts quickly, but developers still test, fix, and make design choices."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e433a6a5-6cc0-4a57-909b-06a7131d82ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2775f1d4-33de-43d3-847e-c715a3082fc3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b86fe448-0879-434d-8a5c-7b42e1b08ac0",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b86fe448-0879-434d-8a5c-7b42e1b08ac0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2775f1d4-33de-43d3-847e-c715a3082fc3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9345ade-b110-40d8-8d3b-59f1cf3e8dcf",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a copy set of the Feature Roulette Cards.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students they'll draw one card at a time and prompt the AI to create that feature or follow the instructions. Encourage them to refer to the Prompting & Versioning panels as needed.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 8. Explain that this is their chance to apply everything they've learned—prompting, refining, and versioning—to build their first AI-assisted web page.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Observe pair as they work. Check if they are successfully refining their prompts and if they understand how to revert to a previous version. Ask probing questions about why they are making certain prompt changes or why they chose to revert such as: \"What did you change in your latest version?\" or \"How are you deciding when your page looks finished?\" or \"What did the AI get right—or wrong—this time?\" Encourage students to compare different AI outputs and think critically about what each version improved or broke.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Building with AI",
        "progression_name": "My First Web Page",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student interactions and final AI-generated pages to gauge understanding of how to apply prompting strategies, refine results, and use version history. Listen for language that shows students can explain their design choices or how they fixed an issue."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that this activity is about experimentation and iteration. Developers rarely get a design perfect on the first try—they refine prompts, fix errors, and test new versions to get closer to their vision. Connect this to professional practice: \"Developers use AI as a creative partner, but they're still responsible for making decisions and improving the final product.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9345ade-b110-40d8-8d3b-59f1cf3e8dcf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2775f1d4-33de-43d3-847e-c715a3082fc3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f1ece66-cb52-4bc6-aac1-5335be6496fd",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to draw on what they noticed while prompting the AI—how it sped up creation and when it needed their refinement or direction. After a minute, invite a few volunteers to share highlights with the class.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the discussion to reinforce the big takeaway: AI can accelerate creation, but developers still guide, test, and refine what it produces. You might ask,\n- Where did the AI make things faster today?\n- When did you still need to step in and make a decision?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f1ece66-cb52-4bc6-aac1-5335be6496fd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7bcc2182-da94-45fb-9a1c-65234227f5f7"
      }
    },
