    },
    {
      "key": "25104c5b-c5f7-4eba-9e85-e22a6455c5bd",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the first of four websites that varies in style (e.g., clean vs. cluttered, playful vs. serious). Ask students to jot down one first-impression word that captures their reaction to the site and one idea for who this website might be designed for. Encourage quick, instinctive responses—students shouldn't overthink.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students hesitate, prompt with: \"What's the very first word that pops into your head?\" Reassure them that there are no wrong answers—every impression is valid."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "25104c5b-c5f7-4eba-9e85-e22a6455c5bd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "28a6a5b2-cd72-4b55-8033-879897143e48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "40c3d2f8-f247-44d6-b430-1ab2601fb2d7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "40c3d2f8-f247-44d6-b430-1ab2601fb2d7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "28a6a5b2-cd72-4b55-8033-879897143e48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce49402b-3ca4-48bd-bc5a-deac47835124",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the second website, which has a playful and colorful style. Ask students to jot down one first-impression word that captures their reaction to the site and one idea for who this website might be designed for. Continue to encourage quick, instinctive responses—students shouldn't overthink.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, ask: \"What design choices made you think this was for a younger audience?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce49402b-3ca4-48bd-bc5a-deac47835124",
        "lesson_activity.key": "28a6a5b2-cd72-4b55-8033-879897143e48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f0aa4888-8ac1-4a58-8541-4d9a79c2467d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f0aa4888-8ac1-4a58-8541-4d9a79c2467d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "28a6a5b2-cd72-4b55-8033-879897143e48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b8c827f3-db17-48bf-ad4d-1775d085071b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the third website, which has an elegant, high-contrast design. Ask students to jot down one first-impression word that captures their reaction to the site and one idea for who this website might be designed for. Continue to encourage quick, instinctive responses.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, ask: \"What design choices—like color, font, or layout—made you think this site was meant for a creative or professional audience?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b8c827f3-db17-48bf-ad4d-1775d085071b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "28a6a5b2-cd72-4b55-8033-879897143e48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1d14eada-b17d-4279-8a6f-8ed0e6c7a33d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1d14eada-b17d-4279-8a6f-8ed0e6c7a33d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "28a6a5b2-cd72-4b55-8033-879897143e48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "db330f47-31c8-44af-8bba-d55f2d8507a9",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the fourth website, which has a warm and cozy design for a small business or café.\nAsk students to jot down one first-impression word that captures their reaction to the site and one idea for who this website might be designed for. Once again, encourage quick, instinctive responses.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, ask: \"What parts of this design—like color, images, or fonts—make it feel welcoming or personal?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "db330f47-31c8-44af-8bba-d55f2d8507a9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "28a6a5b2-cd72-4b55-8033-879897143e48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e60a38bd-7af3-42e1-a216-cea36d20072e",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e60a38bd-7af3-42e1-a216-cea36d20072e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "28a6a5b2-cd72-4b55-8033-879897143e48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "78a88cc1-80aa-4e18-bf7b-f6bc0585b26e",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Gather student responses for each site (use the board, sticky notes, or a digital tool). As students share their first-impression words and ideas for who each site might be for, record patterns such as repeated words, shared audiences, or clear differences between designs.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students only share one-word answers without elaboration, ask a quick follow-up like:\n- \"What about that site made you think it was for that audience?\"\n- \"What design choice made you think of that word?\"\n- \"Do you think most users would see it that way, or was that your personal take?\"\n\nThis keeps the activity brisk but adds depth, helping students connect instinctive impressions and audience guesses to concrete design elements (e.g., color, layout, font, imagery)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "78a88cc1-80aa-4e18-bf7b-f6bc0585b26e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "28a6a5b2-cd72-4b55-8033-879897143e48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aec98d20-5571-462b-8b5f-d7afafcc2225",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aec98d20-5571-462b-8b5f-d7afafcc2225",
        "lesson_activity.key": "28a6a5b2-cd72-4b55-8033-879897143e48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aae0d8f8-f570-4e4b-bcb9-fc9010162def",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Notice how some of your words and audience ideas overlap while others are totally different. That shows how quickly design choices communicate — and how much style affects who a site appeals to.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aae0d8f8-f570-4e4b-bcb9-fc9010162def",
        "lesson_activity.key": "28a6a5b2-cd72-4b55-8033-879897143e48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9b5f31ac-c820-4342-b0d8-b18bd1d734b3",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9b5f31ac-c820-4342-b0d8-b18bd1d734b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "28a6a5b2-cd72-4b55-8033-879897143e48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "30ca68a0-bb90-4cf4-92bd-5874798b2897",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Style Critique activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students annotate the website images in their Style Critique activity guide by labeling Strengths and Weaknesses. Remind them to think about clarity (is it easy to read?), cohesion (do the design elements feel consistent?), and audience fit (does the style match who the site is for?).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, check that they're using the three criteria to explain their annotations. If students write vague notes (e.g., \"bad colors\"), prompt them to be specific (e.g., \"low contrast between text and background makes it hard to read\").",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Style Critique",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Model one example first — circle a feature and label it Strength or Weakness with a short note like \"easy to read\" or \"too crowded\". This shows students that their annotations don't need to be elaborate to be effective."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "30ca68a0-bb90-4cf4-92bd-5874798b2897",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a2448ac7-6c85-4c65-85f5-3132f945d664"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0bb660ed-f844-4e32-b1c9-6ee9d8cee15c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0bb660ed-f844-4e32-b1c9-6ee9d8cee15c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a2448ac7-6c85-4c65-85f5-3132f945d664"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dbfb2694-3531-415a-9cf5-0e2f2f259901",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What made one site's design stronger than another?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to identify specific design strengths (e.g., clear navigation, balanced color scheme, readable text). Help students notice how design choices—like layout, color, or readability—affect how effective a site feels. Use this moment to introduce the idea of competitive analysis (learning by comparing what works and what doesn't in different designs) and establish criteria that students will later apply to their own mood boards.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If discussion stalls, prompt with focused comparisons:\n\n- \"Which site was easier to read, and why?\"\n- \"Which site felt more consistent in style?\"\n- \"Which site seemed to fit its audience best?\"\n\nThis keeps the conversation grounded in observable design choices rather than personal preference."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dbfb2694-3531-415a-9cf5-0e2f2f259901",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a2448ac7-6c85-4c65-85f5-3132f945d664"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "184e8d9b-4f7b-47fb-be55-3bbe2159ec1e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "184e8d9b-4f7b-47fb-be55-3bbe2159ec1e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a2448ac7-6c85-4c65-85f5-3132f945d664"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1681c6a-3fec-437b-9bfd-c74b8a610872",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How do these tools help them test or share their ideas before building the final design?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to see that designers use these tools to explore and communicate style or direction before committing to a final design. Students should understand that these tools allow designers to test ideas quickly, get feedback, and align with teammates or clients on the \"feel\" of a project.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If discussion stalls, prompt with: \"Why might a designer want to try different styles before building the full site?\" or \"How do these tools make collaboration or feedback easier?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1681c6a-3fec-437b-9bfd-c74b8a610872",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a2448ac7-6c85-4c65-85f5-3132f945d664"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a14e540d-b227-443e-9a53-5f8c7bb5aa02",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a14e540d-b227-443e-9a53-5f8c7bb5aa02",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a2448ac7-6c85-4c65-85f5-3132f945d664"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b19df881-2132-4906-be28-43cb3f2bd5eb",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you'll work like visual designers—exploring style and mood before deciding on details.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b19df881-2132-4906-be28-43cb3f2bd5eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a2448ac7-6c85-4c65-85f5-3132f945d664"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2df421fe-7b84-4585-86bc-4216de73cdbb",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define mood boarding as a tool designers use to collect visual inspiration. Emphasize that mood boards are a familiar way to express feeling or style, not to create a finished product.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Mood Boarding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Connect mood boards to contexts students know—like Pinterest boards, vision boards, or even outfit collages. This helps students see mood boards as familiar tools for expressing style choices."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2df421fe-7b84-4585-86bc-4216de73cdbb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a2448ac7-6c85-4c65-85f5-3132f945d664"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3379ed82-6789-479d-aa7f-345cd7f95a36",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 3.\n- Level 1: Students use Sketch Lab to build a mood board that communicates a clear style direction\n- Level 2: Students read the vocabulary definition for mood boarding.\n- Level 3: Students add their own explanation or example of mood boarding to the vocab free response.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are including all key components (colors, fonts/typography, images, and 2–3 style words). Encourage students who are only picking random images/colors to think about how their choices connect to the user experience and the vibe they want to communicate. Ask probing questions like: \"What feeling do you want your user to have when they see this?\" or \"Do these colors and fonts work together as a set?\"",
        "progression_name": "Mood Board Design"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3379ed82-6789-479d-aa7f-345cd7f95a36",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a2448ac7-6c85-4c65-85f5-3132f945d664"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "48f6fd90-6d09-44a9-b76a-16dae76c6aa7",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Mood Boarding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that mood boards don't need to be perfect or polished—they're meant to explore style direction. Suggest that students step back and ask, \"If someone else saw this board, would they instantly get the vibe I'm going for?\""
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this activity as a formative check of how well students can apply design critique concepts to their own work. Look for evidence that students are:\n- Choosing visuals that communicate a consistent mood or theme (clarity and cohesion).\n- Making design choices that fit a user or purpose (audience fit).\n- Using vocabulary like \"color palette,\" \"contrast,\" or \"style\" when explaining their decisions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "48f6fd90-6d09-44a9-b76a-16dae76c6aa7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a2448ac7-6c85-4c65-85f5-3132f945d664"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b592e53-c06e-43b0-a8bd-9ac72307f67e",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b592e53-c06e-43b0-a8bd-9ac72307f67e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a2448ac7-6c85-4c65-85f5-3132f945d664"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9b7ebd17-8824-451c-aaa2-faa0a129581f",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students walk around and look at other mood boards created for the same use case. Explain that this mirrors real design reviews, where teams give each other feedback focused on clarity, cohesion, and audience fit. Direct each student to leave at least one sticky note of feedback using one of the sentence frames.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to give feedback that is both specific and kind. If you notice vague notes like \"Looks good,\" redirect them with:\n- \"What exactly about it communicates the style?\"\n- \"What detail could they adjust to make it stronger?\"\n\nThis keeps feedback actionable and helps students practice constructive critique."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9b7ebd17-8824-451c-aaa2-faa0a129581f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a2448ac7-6c85-4c65-85f5-3132f945d664"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bdedd650-b3fc-4423-8509-045b8f7bb297",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Facilitate a quick reflection using the Three W's."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bdedd650-b3fc-4423-8509-045b8f7bb297",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd6410fc-7bd2-4210-a54e-21822099cba9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e415b7af-0e9c-4e55-b20e-cecf88a37528",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 4 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e415b7af-0e9c-4e55-b20e-cecf88a37528",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd6410fc-7bd2-4210-a54e-21822099cba9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0f26912f-3eae-43e1-b77d-2f41e1345d8f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students experience how colors communicate tone and emotion by categorizing palettes based on mood."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0f26912f-3eae-43e1-b77d-2f41e1345d8f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fcd18a83-9b96-4d2f-a444-70fb2c6013fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d01eef36-bf6a-4411-b1b3-e90756f645a5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Designers use color to make people feel something. Let's see what emotions these palettes create for you.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d01eef36-bf6a-4411-b1b3-e90756f645a5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fcd18a83-9b96-4d2f-a444-70fb2c6013fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f8563bf7-ca74-49eb-9e48-25b68b7daeee",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show 4 color palettes. Ask students to label each of them with one of the categories (energetic, calming, playful, or luxury). Encourage quick, instinctive choices—there are no wrong answers.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage gut reactions and reassure students that there are no wrong answers. The point is to notice how instinctively color communicates mood."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f8563bf7-ca74-49eb-9e48-25b68b7daeee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fcd18a83-9b96-4d2f-a444-70fb2c6013fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8f7676de-aff8-41c8-bb87-dd325d7dab0d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8f7676de-aff8-41c8-bb87-dd325d7dab0d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fcd18a83-9b96-4d2f-a444-70fb2c6013fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a1303102-409a-4781-8407-c3ac0b94de82",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal brand logos or names behind the color palettes after students have labeled them by mood. If students aren't familiar with a brand, have them focus on what the colors communicate: \"What mood or message do these colors send?\" or \"Do they feel consistent with the kind of product or audience this brand might have?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Do these palettes fit the brands shown? Why or why not?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students recognize that colors communicate emotion and identity — and that meaning can change based on culture, audience, or context. Emphasize how color can reinforce or challenge a brand's identity and why inclusive design considers different interpretations."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a1303102-409a-4781-8407-c3ac0b94de82",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fcd18a83-9b96-4d2f-a444-70fb2c6013fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "00ef4dc0-a1ee-4f55-8b3f-38f13121268d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Look at how powerful these colors are. Even without seeing the logo, you made strong guesses about what they meant. When we reveal the brands, sometimes your impressions match, and other times they don't. That shows how designers use color to shape how we see a brand — but color meanings can vary across cultures and accessibility needs. Even designers or AI might assume everyone sees color the same way, which isn't always true.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "00ef4dc0-a1ee-4f55-8b3f-38f13121268d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fcd18a83-9b96-4d2f-a444-70fb2c6013fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f841f2b7-5fb8-4782-a222-671b72328eab",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f841f2b7-5fb8-4782-a222-671b72328eab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fcd18a83-9b96-4d2f-a444-70fb2c6013fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bbcc0d62-6e34-438d-8e1b-c9e808bfb722",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the professional role of a marketing specialist to frame the lesson. Explain that students will take on this role as they analyze existing brands and create their own.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Brand Analysis",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Return to this role when introducing each new activity. Remind students that they're testing ideas and refining designs, just like professionals in real branding teams."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bbcc0d62-6e34-438d-8e1b-c9e808bfb722",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ccdc600-6a8e-4b3b-bca5-e8a8dc4220cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a766fd6-1fcb-48b4-b8de-c2922bb6fbc6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a766fd6-1fcb-48b4-b8de-c2922bb6fbc6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ccdc600-6a8e-4b3b-bca5-e8a8dc4220cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f98fea93-8ca7-423f-8c52-9e6d12188662",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a copy of the Brand Analysis activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students analyze how real websites use fonts and colors to communicate brand identity and audience.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 1 to work with their partner and compare screenshots of websites in panel levels (Nike vs Adidas, Spotify vs SoundCloud, etc.). Students should use their activity guide to record observations on color, typography, and then decided which audience is each brand aiming for.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Make sure students are noting both fonts and colors. Push them past surface-level comments like \"Nike uses black.\" Instead, prompt: \"What feeling does this palette communicate?\" and \"What kind of user do you think they're trying to attract?\"",
        "progression_name": "Web App Comparisons",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Check that students are justifying their claims (e.g., \"Spotify uses playful green and rounded fonts, which feels approachable for younger users\")."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f98fea93-8ca7-423f-8c52-9e6d12188662",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ccdc600-6a8e-4b3b-bca5-e8a8dc4220cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9fc630dc-4c94-47c7-bc18-7227e33f50dc",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9fc630dc-4c94-47c7-bc18-7227e33f50dc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ccdc600-6a8e-4b3b-bca5-e8a8dc4220cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ec983525-bcd0-4767-9742-2999cce8d6e0",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What patterns did you notice in how brands use fonts and colors?*\n- *How did the design choices connect to audience?*\n- *What audiences might feel left out by these design choices?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to identify common design patterns (how fonts and colors communicate tone), connect those choices to the intended audience, and consider who might feel excluded or misrepresented by the same design choices. Encourage reasoning over preference—students explain why design decisions work or don't work for different audiences. If needed, model with examples: Bold sans-serif fonts and high-contrast colors often signal energy or athleticism, while muted serif fonts and gold tones feel more elegant or luxurious. For instance, Nike's dark palette feels powerful and competitive, while Spotify's rounded fonts and bright green feel youthful and approachable.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define brand kit. Briefly point out that a strong brand kit supports clarity, consistency, and accessibility in design."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ec983525-bcd0-4767-9742-2999cce8d6e0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ccdc600-6a8e-4b3b-bca5-e8a8dc4220cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4014ee93-1f77-4e51-ac4f-b7cb0144a2bb",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As marketing specialists, you'll now create your own mini brand kit. Think about how your colors and fonts can tell a clear story, reach your audience, and stay readable for everyone.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4014ee93-1f77-4e51-ac4f-b7cb0144a2bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ccdc600-6a8e-4b3b-bca5-e8a8dc4220cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc1c3f73-0653-46dc-9659-67a925784b9c",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students design a mini brand identity that reflects a user persona's needs by making intentional, inclusive choices about colors and fonts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 2 through 5.\n- Level 2: Students create a mini brand kit for a fictional product and user persona\n- Level 3: Students read the vocabulary definition for brand kit.\n- Level 4: Students add their own explanation or example of brand kit to the vocab free response.\n- Level 5: Students complete a Check for Understanding question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students design, ask reflective questions: \"What mood do these colors communicate?\", \"Would these colors or fonts be easy for all users to read?\", or \"How does your design show awareness of audience or accessibility?\" Remind students to open and use the AI Chat in Level 2b to complete their ethics and accessibility check. Encourage them to reflect on what the AI points out—whether it confirms their design or surfaces new things to consider.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Create a Brand Kit",
        "progression_name": "Create a Brand Kit"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc1c3f73-0653-46dc-9659-67a925784b9c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ccdc600-6a8e-4b3b-bca5-e8a8dc4220cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b70fcd7b-15f1-416d-bf46-384c5d1ba104",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Brand Kit"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b70fcd7b-15f1-416d-bf46-384c5d1ba104",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ccdc600-6a8e-4b3b-bca5-e8a8dc4220cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b09eb3a5-b12a-485c-bed4-d6431b9854d7",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students the goal isn't just to make something that looks good. It's to design intentionally for their persona. Suggest they use tools like Coolors, Google Fonts, or AI for inspiration if stuck. If students' color choices are hard to read, prompt them to adjust and explain why."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students' brand kits as a formative check of the lesson objectives. Look for evidence that students can:\n- Apply design principles to create a cohesive visual identity.\n- Explain and justify how their choices communicate tone and audience.\n- Consider accessibility and inclusivity (e.g., color contrast, readability)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b09eb3a5-b12a-485c-bed4-d6431b9854d7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ccdc600-6a8e-4b3b-bca5-e8a8dc4220cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8eaafa9b-e6ac-4758-9384-c24959e916b9",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8eaafa9b-e6ac-4758-9384-c24959e916b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ccdc600-6a8e-4b3b-bca5-e8a8dc4220cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31f199e8-a301-4acb-b292-1722d0ffc387",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Give students practice articulating and defending design choices in terms of audience needs, accessibility, and inclusivity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct groups to present their brand kits to a peer group with the same fictional product. Each group explains their reasoning using the provided sentence frames. Encourage students to respond to peer feedback by explaining why they made a specific choice.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for groups clearly tying color and typography choices to audience needs. If students only describe the palette, push with: \"Why does that choice matter for your persona?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "As groups present, listen for evidence that students can:\n- Explain how design choices communicate tone and identity.\n- Justify how their choices serve their user persona.\n- Acknowledge accessibility or inclusivity in their reasoning."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31f199e8-a301-4acb-b292-1722d0ffc387",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ccdc600-6a8e-4b3b-bca5-e8a8dc4220cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "283f85c9-953e-40d9-94aa-9c15abc6b304",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "283f85c9-953e-40d9-94aa-9c15abc6b304",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ccdc600-6a8e-4b3b-bca5-e8a8dc4220cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "228e4312-bff3-4bcb-8357-afb2afee8e6f",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does your design avoid stereotypes or bias?*\n- *How might users from different backgrounds interpret your color and font choices?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on how design choices communicate identity and values while considering inclusivity and diverse perspectives. They connect visual decisions (color, font, tone) to ethical design—recognizing that different users may interpret designs differently based on culture, experience, or ability."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "228e4312-bff3-4bcb-8357-afb2afee8e6f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3ccdc600-6a8e-4b3b-bca5-e8a8dc4220cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fadd806e-9e3a-4ea9-a73e-cc66941807b1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students share their reflection responses from Level 5: Check for Understanding or display a few anonymous responses to share aloud.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As students share, highlight reflections that connect to:\n- Intentional design — making choices for a clear purpose and audience.\n- Accessibility and inclusivity — ensuring everyone can engage with the design.\n- Ethical awareness — understanding that both people and AI can carry bias into creative decisions.\n\nIf time allows, end with a short whole-class question: \"How can we design responsibly when we use AI to make creative choices?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fadd806e-9e3a-4ea9-a73e-cc66941807b1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e176916d-d2cb-43b4-913f-cb24cc546fb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "647aa7c7-8482-4529-a0ff-df000829331b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "647aa7c7-8482-4529-a0ff-df000829331b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e176916d-d2cb-43b4-913f-cb24cc546fb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a1c7d6a7-debf-47db-9532-a86efb71d142",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a1c7d6a7-debf-47db-9532-a86efb71d142",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e176916d-d2cb-43b4-913f-cb24cc546fb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "309663d2-2be3-4c2e-b9e6-0352d305cf5e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 6 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "309663d2-2be3-4c2e-b9e6-0352d305cf5e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e176916d-d2cb-43b4-913f-cb24cc546fb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "37f5e313-744e-428a-81e4-9fa39d6830b3",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students quickly recall the difference between HTML and CSS by visualizing what happens when styling is removed. They connect this idea to brand clarity and user experience — realizing that CSS gives identity and consistency to a site.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show a webpage styled with CSS. Pose the question: \"If we removed the CSS stylesheet, what would be left?\" Students sketch a quick version of the same page without CSS and annotate their sketches with reasoning (e.g., \"Text still shows but without color,\" \"Images stack vertically,\" \"No clear brand identity\"). After sketching, have a few students share what they noticed. Encourage students to connect their observations to how CSS communicates tone and brand consistency.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for misconceptions like \"nothing will show.\" Prompt with: \"Would the content still exist without CSS?\" or \"What happens to layout and colors if CSS is gone?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does CSS turn a brand kit or design idea into a real web page?*\n- *What do developers and designers need to communicate clearly when styling a page?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should connect their understanding of CSS to real-world design workflows. The goal is to get students to recognize that CSS isn't just about color or font — it's about translating design decisions into code and ensuring collaboration between designers and developers."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "37f5e313-744e-428a-81e4-9fa39d6830b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "87e2398f-4777-483e-96c4-f631cd07086b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9d36c71-13bb-42bc-9758-76c04a65df32",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When a designer creates a layout or brand kit, it's not automatically ready for the web. CSS is how front-end developers bring those visuals to life — they translate colors, spacing, and typography into code. This handoff between design and development is part of what you'll practice today — testing how well AI follows design instructions.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9d36c71-13bb-42bc-9758-76c04a65df32",
        "lesson_activity.key": "87e2398f-4777-483e-96c4-f631cd07086b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7c075392-c30b-4da1-8f61-6e6cb8662ac1",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7c075392-c30b-4da1-8f61-6e6cb8662ac1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "87e2398f-4777-483e-96c4-f631cd07086b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d320bf42-22f2-4315-9eee-cf9fce00e85a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of the Activity: Establish students' role as front-end web designers who translate brand kits into live, coded web pages using CSS.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that students will act as front-end designers using AI as their coding partner. Designers define the vision, and front-end developers ensure it's executed correctly in code — responsibly, accessibly, and on brand.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Style Sheets",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Refer back to this role throughout the activity. Remind students they're testing and refining AI-generated CSS like professionals validating a build."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d320bf42-22f2-4315-9eee-cf9fce00e85a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fce35dea-78f3-4a9b-90b6-8d47f361ada0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a0a8bd6-14b3-4766-a84e-90a19cb70a1b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define style sheet. Explain that CSS is the bridge between design and development: it's how the decisions made in a brand kit — like colors, fonts, and layout — become consistent code. Every time students see a website where the colors, fonts, and spacing look intentional and consistent, that's CSS at work.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of the Activity: Students apply a stylesheet across multiple pages to maintain consistent brand identity, testing and refining AI-generated code for accuracy and readability.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 7.\n- Level 1: Students learn how to add images as context for their AI prompts.\n- Level 2: Students learn how to add code as context for their AI prompts.\n- Level 3: Students learn how to add items to and retrieve items from the Backpack.\n- Level 4: Students prompt AI to generate and apply a basic CSS stylesheet.\n- Level 5: Students prompt AI to apply brand colors and fonts to key regions (header, nav, main, footer) and refine for accuracy.\n- Level 6: Students read the vocabulary definition for style sheet.\n- Level 7: Students add their own explanation or example of style sheet to the vocab free response.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students provided the stylesheet as context to the AI and prompted the AI to generate new HTML with their stylesheet linked in the <head>). Have students explain what they notice about the AI's code and why it made those styling choices.",
        "progression_name": "How To: Adding Context and Saving Prompts"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a0a8bd6-14b3-4766-a84e-90a19cb70a1b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fce35dea-78f3-4a9b-90b6-8d47f361ada0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4d94c164-9f83-4d8f-b207-d562074942f0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Apply a Stylesheet"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4d94c164-9f83-4d8f-b207-d562074942f0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fce35dea-78f3-4a9b-90b6-8d47f361ada0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fbd9cf5e-0823-4872-93f8-6d86ed9d443f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Stylesheet",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to \"debug like a developer.\" If the AI's output doesn't look right, they should describe why instead of just retrying the prompt. This models safe, reflective use of AI tools — not just trust in automation.\nIf time allows, connect back to the Front-End Designer role: \"This is the testing phase — where developers validate the AI's work before the site goes live.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fbd9cf5e-0823-4872-93f8-6d86ed9d443f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fce35dea-78f3-4a9b-90b6-8d47f361ada0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "521cf3db-bb82-4790-8f95-3a6617e7f44d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What did the AI change in your CSS — and why?*\n- *Did it make any styling choices you wouldn't have made yourself?*\n- *How do you know if its output matches your brand goals?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on how AI interprets prompts and makes automated styling decisions. They consider when AI-generated CSS aligns with their intent and when it diverges. The goal is for students to see themselves as responsible reviewers, not passive users — understanding that AI can enhance efficiency but still requires human judgment for accuracy, accessibility, and design intent."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "521cf3db-bb82-4790-8f95-3a6617e7f44d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fce35dea-78f3-4a9b-90b6-8d47f361ada0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5a669859-60f0-41f9-b85a-d5162b82e58c",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When developers use AI tools, they don't assume every output is correct — they check what changed, why it changed, and whether it still fits the brand's tone and user needs. You're doing the same thing here: validating the AI's choices.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5a669859-60f0-41f9-b85a-d5162b82e58c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fce35dea-78f3-4a9b-90b6-8d47f361ada0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1a76d342-9d86-48f7-ac39-ee6be14ce529",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of the Activity: Students demonstrate mastery by refining and experimenting with CSS using AI assistance — improving accuracy, readability, and brand consistency.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Have students go to Level 8 and choose one challenge to complete. Encourage them to think like developers in a testing phase: What small refinements could make your page more polished, consistent, or accessible?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, check that they:\n- Understand what their AI-generated CSS is doing before changing it.\n- Test updates across multiple elements (e.g., headers, buttons, cards).\n- Document changes and explain their reasoning",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Style Choice Challenge",
        "progression_name": "Styling Challenge",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "- If students finish early, invite them to complete an additional challenge or revisit earlier CSS for readability improvements.\n- Encourage students to use before-and-after comparisons to visualize their improvements — either by commenting changes in their code or using screenshots. This helps them see the impact of refining CSS and builds professional documentation habits."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this challenge to formatively assess whether students can:\n- Apply CSS intentionally to maintain brand consistency.\n- Identify and correct AI-generated errors (e.g., low contrast, inconsistent margins, wrong font).\n- Explain changes clearly using plain, professional language."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1a76d342-9d86-48f7-ac39-ee6be14ce529",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fce35dea-78f3-4a9b-90b6-8d47f361ada0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "44649476-c99e-4c0a-b42a-03be1e24cf64",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite students to reflect on their learning by responding to the prompts on the slide. Ask them to share quick responses out loud or record them on sticky notes, a shared board, or a digital tool. Help students connect today's lesson to the larger story of how design becomes code. They should recognize that CSS translates brand identity into real web pages, and that AI acts as a coding assistant, not an automatic expert.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "44649476-c99e-4c0a-b42a-03be1e24cf64",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c88dcc0e-31c3-4e87-893c-6279299d48a9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8046011b-3074-489c-9923-be3407668be8",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 9 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8046011b-3074-489c-9923-be3407668be8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c88dcc0e-31c3-4e87-893c-6279299d48a9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e2adf733-db2e-4bac-8306-4e3152680215",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Have students activate empathy by connecting unmet technology needs with emotions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Think of a time when technology didn't meet your needs. In one word, how did it make you feel?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students see that unmet needs carry emotional weight (frustration, confusion, being left out) and that emotions are part of user needs."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e2adf733-db2e-4bac-8306-4e3152680215",
        "lesson_activity.key": "063be737-22a2-4847-b87c-a05fedb7fed1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb76e45f-2123-4de2-aa21-291312071f89",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When we build personas and problem statements today, we want to capture not just what users do, but how they feel. If we understand needs and emotions clearly, we can design solutions that don't leave users frustrated.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb76e45f-2123-4de2-aa21-291312071f89",
        "lesson_activity.key": "063be737-22a2-4847-b87c-a05fedb7fed1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "194f81d9-d88b-47f2-a6a0-861a4898c63e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Analyze a persona from a series and connect it to design implications.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give each student a copy of the Design Implications activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 and 2.\n- Level 1: Read an informational panel on how user personas inform design decisions\n- Level 2: Analyze a sample user persona, inferring design decisions and connecting them to a problem statement. Complete page 1 of the activity guide.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "User Persona Analysis",
        "progression_name": "User Personas and Problem Statements"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "194f81d9-d88b-47f2-a6a0-861a4898c63e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5257fa4a-0783-43c3-a837-c90515b071ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "78d2a6a2-1a93-4eb6-b490-b4ed69b45433",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "User Persona Analysis: Alex"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "78d2a6a2-1a93-4eb6-b490-b4ed69b45433",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5257fa4a-0783-43c3-a837-c90515b071ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9682e916-cc85-4f5e-8123-34426b04b6aa",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define problem statement. Problem statement format - [user] needs a way to [need] because [reason].\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which statement is stronger? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that B is better because it's specific, user-centered, and explains constraints."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9682e916-cc85-4f5e-8123-34426b04b6aa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5257fa4a-0783-43c3-a837-c90515b071ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b3d5fc2-3ddc-4b8c-a9e2-f06d92746cbf",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Students create a user persona and write problem statements with AI support.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 3. Students should choose a scenario, create a sample user persona, and draft a problem statement.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Create a User Persona",
        "progression_name": "Create a User Persona and Problem Statement"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b3d5fc2-3ddc-4b8c-a9e2-f06d92746cbf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5257fa4a-0783-43c3-a837-c90515b071ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "34e0f896-1b6d-4a05-8781-9de36a81e015",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students swap personas and problem statements with a partner to provide feedback. Students use a Peer Review Checklist to provide feedback.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for feedback that is specific and actionable.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 4 and 5.\n- Level 4: Review the vocabulary term problem statement.\n- Level 5: Define the term in student's own words. Use examples from the lesson to explain their thinking.",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Problem Statement"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "34e0f896-1b6d-4a05-8781-9de36a81e015",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5257fa4a-0783-43c3-a837-c90515b071ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "722ec9ac-bc2d-48e0-b878-08624ebd9d8c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "722ec9ac-bc2d-48e0-b878-08624ebd9d8c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81bc3872-8936-456c-b296-dfbee35130da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "990ee183-bf36-47fe-ab5a-f4dbd46d78bf",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 6 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "990ee183-bf36-47fe-ab5a-f4dbd46d78bf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81bc3872-8936-456c-b296-dfbee35130da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1501adc2-509c-46ab-b78a-7d7197c3f50d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Students practice decomposition by organizing messy information into a clear, logical structure before learning the term site map.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two or three,\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a set of the Website cards."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1501adc2-509c-46ab-b78a-7d7197c3f50d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9486dec0-f53f-4b36-a826-c8819c705429"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f059727e-f4e1-4811-86cf-f7e0c7e4c77a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Your client's website is confusing! Users can't find what they need. How might you reorganize the pages so that navigation feels clear and logical?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f059727e-f4e1-4811-86cf-f7e0c7e4c77a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9486dec0-f53f-4b36-a826-c8819c705429"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f747b946-78e9-4ed2-9590-d2ff0afb9466",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct groups to arrange the 12 page cards into a logical structure, using arrows to show how users would move through the site.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Move between groups and ask why they grouped pages a certain way. Encourage reasoning such as \"We put Contact under About because users usually look for it with company info.\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Let students know that the example on the slide is not the \"correct answer.\" There's no single right way to organize a site — what matters is whether the structure makes sense for the user. Encourage groups to explain their reasoning and compare different logical choices."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f747b946-78e9-4ed2-9590-d2ff0afb9466",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9486dec0-f53f-4b36-a826-c8819c705429"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2ae9ee38-d444-4174-8aea-ac084f45145f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2ae9ee38-d444-4174-8aea-ac084f45145f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9486dec0-f53f-4b36-a826-c8819c705429"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0fe29328-dfd3-49b2-92a1-ac24641f1b69",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define site map. Explain that site maps help us see the overall structure and logic of a site — not just the pages themselves but how they relate. Tell students that now that they have explored what makes navigation clear, they will create their own structured site map later in today's activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What patterns do we notice across site maps?*\n  - *Where did most groups put Home?*\n  - *Where did most groups put Contact?*\n  - *How did groups handle sections or sub-pages?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to surface shared principles of effective site maps, such as: clear hierarchy (Home at the top), consistent placement of key pages, logical grouping of related content. Capture a few criteria on the board for reference in later activities.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students mention unusual or creative structures, validate them — use these examples to discuss when breaking convention can help or hurt usability."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0fe29328-dfd3-49b2-92a1-ac24641f1b69",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9486dec0-f53f-4b36-a826-c8819c705429"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a798f634-3070-4c7a-878d-9d3935039e41",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a798f634-3070-4c7a-878d-9d3935039e41",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9486dec0-f53f-4b36-a826-c8819c705429"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ed5348c-cafd-43e2-af30-8699c16b39b6",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ed5348c-cafd-43e2-af30-8699c16b39b6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9486dec0-f53f-4b36-a826-c8819c705429"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5731b6ee-e1c6-463b-aa64-beaa3566a003",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of Activity: Connect site mapping to real-world roles in tech and show how UX Designers plan user experiences before coding begins.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly spotlight careers such as UX Designer or Information Architect. Explain that these roles are responsible for structuring site navigation and ensuring users can find what they need. Emphasize that today's activity mirrors that real-world process — planning first, building later.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "User Experience Design",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Invite students to name an app or website that feels easy to navigate. Ask, \"What do you think the designer did to make that happen?\" This grounds the role in tangible examples students know."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5731b6ee-e1c6-463b-aa64-beaa3566a003",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4736cf82-499f-4def-934f-308d952ceee0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0bb4e9ba-2a0b-4ae4-8d47-f12cf9bd73e8",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0bb4e9ba-2a0b-4ae4-8d47-f12cf9bd73e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4736cf82-499f-4def-934f-308d952ceee0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "64e3b219-232b-4d9a-b77f-700f3eb1985a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As UX Designers, part of your job is planning how users move through a site to complete a goal — that's called a user flow. For example, if you're designing a food delivery app, the user flow might be: Home → Menu → Add to Cart → Checkout. Today, you'll create your own user flow for your app idea to make sure navigation makes sense before anything is built.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "64e3b219-232b-4d9a-b77f-700f3eb1985a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4736cf82-499f-4def-934f-308d952ceee0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a1cfc2cc-6127-42c4-b080-15ad891bc2db",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define user flow. Explain that they'll map one today as part of their site map challenge.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 7.\n- Levels 1 and 2: Students create a site map and trace a user flow for a chosen persona.\n- Levels 3 through 6: Students read and explain the key terms site map and user flow.\n- Level 7: Students respond to a final reflection or Check for Understanding question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that groups are creating nested structures rather than flat lists. Prompt them with questions like, \"Would a user know where to find this page?\" and \"Does this structure help your persona reach their goal easily?\"",
        "progression_name": "Create a Site Map and User Flow"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a1cfc2cc-6127-42c4-b080-15ad891bc2db",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4736cf82-499f-4def-934f-308d952ceee0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aaf0aceb-ca5b-498e-bed2-dd480b74c069",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Site Map"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aaf0aceb-ca5b-498e-bed2-dd480b74c069",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4736cf82-499f-4def-934f-308d952ceee0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82772ffb-6cfc-4b67-ab4a-6c6cbc02bb8d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: User Flow"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82772ffb-6cfc-4b67-ab4a-6c6cbc02bb8d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4736cf82-499f-4def-934f-308d952ceee0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a10aee8-b056-414c-9ee4-db7961e017b2",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "- Remind students to consider their persona's goals and challenges as they trace the flow. Does the flow make sense for that user?\n- Remind students that there isn't one \"right\" site structure — what matters is how well it serves their persona's needs. Encourage them to explain why their flow makes sense for that user."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use levels 1 and 2 to formatively assess student progress on lesson objectives. Check whether students can:\n- Creating a site map showing page hierarchy and navigation\n- Tracing a possible user flow across a web app."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a10aee8-b056-414c-9ee4-db7961e017b2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4736cf82-499f-4def-934f-308d952ceee0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a9fcfd99-6e73-4481-b4a7-09464bb5217f",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a9fcfd99-6e73-4481-b4a7-09464bb5217f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4736cf82-499f-4def-934f-308d952ceee0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7877feb-de9f-459d-a14d-ee9abecbd639",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Before we hand our site maps to the AI, let's think like designers and engineers. When we give instructions to an AI, it only knows what we tell it — it doesn't automatically understand our intent. Let's talk about how that affects the way we explain our designs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7877feb-de9f-459d-a14d-ee9abecbd639",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4736cf82-499f-4def-934f-308d952ceee0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9b472ea1-1afb-4f40-b4c9-68d7b4d8f8f1",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does your site map make navigation clear and support user needs?*\n- *How do we explain our site's logic to AI so it builds what we intend?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to connect human-centered design and AI collaboration. Students should recognize that:\n- A clear site map helps users navigate and achieve goals easily.\n- Communicating that structure clearly helps the AI generate more accurate, user-friendly designs.\n\nEncourage students to explain their reasoning using examples: \"We put Help under Contact because that's where users expect it.\" and \"If we don't tell the AI why a section is important, it might skip it or place it wrong.\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, ask one more additional question: What do you need to make sure the AI understands before it starts building? This primes students for precision and transparency — a great segue into the next task."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9b472ea1-1afb-4f40-b4c9-68d7b4d8f8f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4736cf82-499f-4def-934f-308d952ceee0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e046d11-ab5b-440b-9977-07f5639af6bf",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e046d11-ab5b-440b-9977-07f5639af6bf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4736cf82-499f-4def-934f-308d952ceee0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f3470a77-6dfb-4c49-9936-40e0c339be9a",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of Activity: Students apply their site map and user flow to generate a working, multi-page website using AI. They practice writing clear, structured prompts that communicate their logic and verify whether the AI builds what they intended.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Have students complete Level 8. Explain that this is their AI hand-off phase—they'll take their plan and test whether the AI can interpret it accurately just like a UX Designer communicating with a development team. Remind them that AI doesn't \"see\" their reasoning—it can only follow what they describe clearly in the prompt. Encourage students to revise their prompt if something doesn't look right — this models iteration and precision in AI collaboration.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Prompt students with questions that build reflection and clarity: \"Does your website structure match your site map?\", \"What might you reword in your prompt to make the AI's output clearer?\", or \"If a link doesn't work, what does that tell you about how the AI interpreted your instructions?\"",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Articulate for AI",
        "progression_name": "Build Your Web App with AI",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Show or read aloud the optional sample prompt to model precision. Emphasize that this isn't a script — students can adapt the wording as long as they're describing structure, navigation, and clarity."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this level to formatively assess student progress on lesson objectives. Check whether students can:\n- Translate design logic into precise, testable AI prompts (clarity and structure in communication).\n- Evaluate AI output for accuracy and alignment with their original site map and user flow.\n- Iterate thoughtfully — making targeted prompt revisions based on observed gaps or misinterpretations."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f3470a77-6dfb-4c49-9936-40e0c339be9a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4736cf82-499f-4def-934f-308d952ceee0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "502359c7-fcda-455e-aac6-c86bcffcd1fa",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *When we prompted the AI, how did we make sure it understood our site's logic?*\n- *What made your structure easier or harder for the AI to get right?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to reflect on what made their site map clear and how effectively they communicated that logic to the AI. Students should recognize that:\n- Clear, organized structure helps both users and AI follow site logic.\n- Precise prompts and explicit relationships (like navigation links) improve AI accuracy.\n- Iterating on prompts is part of refining and testing how well AI interprets design intent.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "502359c7-fcda-455e-aac6-c86bcffcd1fa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da0ee800-991d-4f37-9270-94bad8f702e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2ba5a5bc-d192-4f70-ba1d-922d6a4f2b90",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 9 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2ba5a5bc-d192-4f70-ba1d-922d6a4f2b90",
        "lesson_activity.key": "da0ee800-991d-4f37-9270-94bad8f702e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4bd1781c-ca2e-42e5-ae3a-30b0cc531597",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students build empathy for users with different visual abilities and recognize how design choices—like color, contrast, and text size—impact accessibility. They connect these challenges to how both human designers and AI tools must think differently to create inclusive web apps."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4bd1781c-ca2e-42e5-ae3a-30b0cc531597",
        "lesson_activity.key": "389982bf-233a-476c-94ca-dbf1e0442321"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "077c6763-1051-4675-a622-1813eb13d756",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we'll step into the shoes of users who see the web differently and explore how both people and AI systems can design for accessibility.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "077c6763-1051-4675-a622-1813eb13d756",
        "lesson_activity.key": "389982bf-233a-476c-94ca-dbf1e0442321"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7622ac6e-93dd-4a60-856f-f947f4877d33",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"Low Vision Experience\" and “Color Blindness Experience” videos.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What stood out about the challenges these users faced?*\n- *What mistakes might AI make if it doesn't consider these challenges?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify specific usability barriers, such as difficulty reading small or low-contrast text, confusing navigation, or reliance on color alone. Students should explain how these issues affect a user's experience (for example, not being able to find a button or read important text). Students should recognize that AI might reproduce inaccessible patterns — for instance, choosing poor color contrast, omitting alt text, or generating layouts that assume perfect vision.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "You can also provide students with the link to Project Lima and have them experience these accessibility issues firsthand. Have students choose at least one of the three experiences to try. Encourage them to spend a few minutes exploring the chosen experience(s). Observe students as they engage with the simulations. After exploring, ask: What was the hardest part of that experience? or How did that simulation make you feel as a user? Connect their emotions to the earlier questions about AI and design responsibility."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7622ac6e-93dd-4a60-856f-f947f4877d33",
        "lesson_activity.key": "389982bf-233a-476c-94ca-dbf1e0442321"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9a983cc9-8cd7-46e4-9beb-9e332fdce929",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9a983cc9-8cd7-46e4-9beb-9e332fdce929",
        "lesson_activity.key": "389982bf-233a-476c-94ca-dbf1e0442321"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e18c913f-cde6-4bab-b97a-4b4cb2f7ac89",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e18c913f-cde6-4bab-b97a-4b4cb2f7ac89",
        "lesson_activity.key": "389982bf-233a-476c-94ca-dbf1e0442321"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "53b60c90-5742-4c01-957d-a966b350c831",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students are introduced to the accessibility challenges that exist across the web and learn how those issues influence AI-generated designs. They explore the real-world role of the Inclusive Designer and understand why human judgment is essential in guiding AI toward more accessible outcomes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the key stat aloud to emphasize its scale. Then, highlight the examples of common issues (low contrast text, missing alt text, unlabeled forms, empty links/buttons).",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "How Accessible is the Web?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "53b60c90-5742-4c01-957d-a966b350c831",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96c2cb47-ca6a-4443-b88d-339c615da9da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "65c3b27f-3bf2-486b-80d1-a811f67c8e10",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI design tools are trained on the same web we just looked at, which means they inherit its strengths and its flaws. Let's talk about what that could mean for accessibility.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "65c3b27f-3bf2-486b-80d1-a811f67c8e10",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96c2cb47-ca6a-4443-b88d-339c615da9da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aff4d869-c41b-4fe0-bd80-8189b80b4a49",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *If most websites have accessibility issues, what might AI learn from that data?*\n- *How could those patterns show up in AI-generated layouts or code?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that AI systems learn from existing examples on the web. If most of those examples are inaccessible, the AI will also learn and reproduce those same barriers. Students should start connecting data quality to AI output quality. Students should identify concrete ways inaccessibility could appear in AI work. Students should see that AI can generate polished-looking designs that still fail real users.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Inclusive Designer role.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the “Thinking Accessibility in UX Design” video."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aff4d869-c41b-4fe0-bd80-8189b80b4a49",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96c2cb47-ca6a-4443-b88d-339c615da9da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "efb433b1-cbd3-43de-a300-679b0ca04f74",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you'll get to practice thinking like an Inclusive Designer. You'll look at an AI-generated webpage, test it for accessibility, and then use what you find to guide the AI toward better, more inclusive design choices.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "efb433b1-cbd3-43de-a300-679b0ca04f74",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96c2cb47-ca6a-4443-b88d-339c615da9da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "531af45e-3e47-4c2b-a483-f6fc08c1de80",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students learn how to use the POUR framework (Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust) to describe and organize accessibility issues they observed in AI-generated layouts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student/pair of students a copy of the Accessibility in Design activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define the POUR Principles.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 3.\n- Levels 1 and 2: Students review and define POUR Principle.\n- Level 3: Students identify at least two to three accessibility issues on the provided web page.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Observe students as they examine the page. Prompt them with questions like: 'What POUR principle does this issue relate to?' or 'How might this issue affect a user with low vision?' Ensure they are focusing on issues like low contrast text, missing alt text, and tiny buttons. Ensure students use the Accessibility Prompt Builder in their Unit Guide to write AI prompts that suggest fixes for the issues they identified.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "The POUR Principle"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "531af45e-3e47-4c2b-a483-f6fc08c1de80",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96c2cb47-ca6a-4443-b88d-339c615da9da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2aabbdb4-336d-467b-8fbc-2614cff3900b",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: POUR Principles"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2aabbdb4-336d-467b-8fbc-2614cff3900b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96c2cb47-ca6a-4443-b88d-339c615da9da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7bc6f406-3fb6-49a1-8f01-34bd2c703cfa",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Fix Accessibility Issues",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students were absent for the previous lesson where they created site maps/apps, make sure they are aware of the AI Prompt in the instructions that will provide a site map for them. Emphasize that the goal is not necessarily to 'solve' every issue, but to practice identifying problems and communicating them effectively through prompts. This is a key skill for working with AI and for accessibility design in general."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7bc6f406-3fb6-49a1-8f01-34bd2c703cfa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96c2cb47-ca6a-4443-b88d-339c615da9da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f419c941-d814-4612-a729-91e41a727347",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f419c941-d814-4612-a729-91e41a727347",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96c2cb47-ca6a-4443-b88d-339c615da9da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "90415f72-331d-485a-94b5-f5af738fa331",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why do we need a framework like POUR when designing for accessibility?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should understand that POUR provides a structured approach to ensure digital products are usable by everyone. Guide them to see it as a checklist or a set of guiding principles that help designers systematically consider different aspects of accessibility. Emphasize that it helps ensure inclusivity and usability across a wide range of user needs and abilities."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "90415f72-331d-485a-94b5-f5af738fa331",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96c2cb47-ca6a-4443-b88d-339c615da9da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ea06ee9-6a0a-4ae7-b5d4-fc84252f63fd",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now we'll use these principles to start identifying issues and prompting AI to fix them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ea06ee9-6a0a-4ae7-b5d4-fc84252f63fd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96c2cb47-ca6a-4443-b88d-339c615da9da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e540d13a-e954-4a5a-91b6-8bb0a23c17bc",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 4. Students apply accessibility principles to their own apps from Lesson 8 by revisiting them with a user persona who has an accessibility constraint.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Remind them to be specific in their prompts, referencing the POUR principles. Ask students to explain how their user's constraint makes certain aspects of their app challenging. Prompt them to connect specific design elements to potential accessibility barriers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 5."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e540d13a-e954-4a5a-91b6-8bb0a23c17bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96c2cb47-ca6a-4443-b88d-339c615da9da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b2d58a3-b57a-4f25-934c-2258291a5719",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Apply Accessibility Principles"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b2d58a3-b57a-4f25-934c-2258291a5719",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96c2cb47-ca6a-4443-b88d-339c615da9da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dce5ddda-7dd9-4dce-a035-eb0d23e8e958",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to connect their suggested change to the idea of empathy and how would this change make life easier for their user."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "This prompt can serve as a formative assessment. Collect student responses to gauge their understanding of applying the POUR framework and user-centered design principles."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dce5ddda-7dd9-4dce-a035-eb0d23e8e958",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96c2cb47-ca6a-4443-b88d-339c615da9da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8c68dd2f-7b91-446e-831f-1ddea19e18cb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8c68dd2f-7b91-446e-831f-1ddea19e18cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d9e1c944-0bd4-443d-a9dc-6f69369e3639"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ea18e9d-e983-4006-a43d-2538bd81f382",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 5 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ea18e9d-e983-4006-a43d-2538bd81f382",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d9e1c944-0bd4-443d-a9dc-6f69369e3639"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ddf36c38-0ede-40e7-8e6e-fa5f4a68be83",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students experience the purpose of quick, low-fidelity sketches by focusing on structure and function rather than visual design. This reinforces abstraction (ignoring unnecessary details), decomposition (breaking the page into parts), and pattern recognition (noticing common layouts)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ddf36c38-0ede-40e7-8e6e-fa5f4a68be83",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f97b683-d880-4acb-862f-b5c8ef14e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b837f361-c64d-4baf-a01b-2e5700305034",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Designers often start by sketching rough layouts to plan how information will be organized. The goal right now is speed and clarity — not polished design. Focus on the big pieces that make a homepage work: navigation, sections, and where content will go.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b837f361-c64d-4baf-a01b-2e5700305034",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f97b683-d880-4acb-862f-b5c8ef14e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e880f233-fe17-4de5-a73c-ce941ae42391",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students one minute to sketch a rough home page for a fictional music playlist app. It should include basic navigation, key sections, a short paragraph, and an image gallery.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize speed over perfection. Encourage students to use rough lines, boxes, and placeholders. They should avoid details like colors, specific photos, or exact wording. This keeps attention on structure — a core idea in abstraction."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e880f233-fe17-4de5-a73c-ce941ae42391",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f97b683-d880-4acb-862f-b5c8ef14e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "830b2620-1a3c-4423-b24c-3789a29aba94",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "830b2620-1a3c-4423-b24c-3789a29aba94",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f97b683-d880-4acb-862f-b5c8ef14e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eab6be29-05de-4411-b92b-9e8bfeb2307f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students one minute to sketch a rough About page for the same fictional music playlist app. The page should include a short description of the app, a section for team or creator information, and navigation that links back to the homepage.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As students sketch the second page, ask them to reuse layout patterns — designers look for consistency across pages to improve navigation and flow. Remind students that they are focusing on structure and layout, not visual design — this continues the practice of abstraction (ignoring details that don't affect structure) and decomposition (breaking each page into functional sections)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eab6be29-05de-4411-b92b-9e8bfeb2307f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f97b683-d880-4acb-862f-b5c8ef14e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "22f49981-a7e6-4bde-944f-63c2cde9d877",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "22f49981-a7e6-4bde-944f-63c2cde9d877",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f97b683-d880-4acb-862f-b5c8ef14e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7193fe49-1a3a-41dd-92ca-edb0df06fe0a",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What choices did you make about what to include or leave out?*\n- *Which parts of your sketch feel like human design choices, and which could AI handle later?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to reflect on how they made structural decisions (abstraction) and how these choices connect to the designer's role in a human-in-the-loop workflow. Students should recognize that:\n- Sketching focuses on what's essential for structure and function, not polished visuals.\n- Designers make early layout and content decisions before AI or developers build — that's what keeps humans in control of the creative process."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7193fe49-1a3a-41dd-92ca-edb0df06fe0a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f97b683-d880-4acb-862f-b5c8ef14e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "064664ae-ac6d-4423-8eef-0b23ff0e1690",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When designers start a project, they make big decisions about structure and function — not about visuals or colors. That's because AI or developers can handle those details later. What you just created is actually your first wireframe — a quick sketch that helps plan structure before any visuals or code come into play. Let's take a look at what wireframes include — and what they leave out.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "064664ae-ac6d-4423-8eef-0b23ff0e1690",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f97b683-d880-4acb-862f-b5c8ef14e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1532b869-a224-4e87-959d-3b79ae943dab",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define wireframe as a low-fidelity, structural layout showing content and functionality without colors, images, or visual styling. Use the blueprint analogy: just like an architect sketches a blueprint before building, designers create wireframes to plan structure and layout before adding visuals. Explain that this step keeps humans in the loop — designers decide structure and logic, while AI or developers handle styling and details later.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Ask students what other kinds of jobs use \"blueprints\" or rough drafts before building the final version. This helps them connect the idea of planning structure first — a core design principle — to the broader concept of human decision-making before automation."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1532b869-a224-4e87-959d-3b79ae943dab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f97b683-d880-4acb-862f-b5c8ef14e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d509f922-9e2b-4652-9dac-bf38c8047bed",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d509f922-9e2b-4652-9dac-bf38c8047bed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f97b683-d880-4acb-862f-b5c8ef14e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3f8309f-689e-4369-af6c-ffa0d2c92f7e",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3f8309f-689e-4369-af6c-ffa0d2c92f7e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f97b683-d880-4acb-862f-b5c8ef14e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "65d3a569-181d-471d-b986-ed4f353185c6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students create low-fidelity wireframes for multiple pages of a single app, ensuring consistency in key UI elements. They map a logical user flow and think like UX Designers deciding structure before AI builds.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the video \"UI Design: When to Wireframe\"\n\nQuestions to consider with video:\n- Why do UX Designers start with sketches before designing?\n- What problems could skipping this step create later?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students understand that wireframing is about clarity and complexity, not polish. Students should recognize that:\n- Pros use sketches to think through structure and user flow before design.\n- Wireframing saves time by revealing layout or usability issues early.\n- This early planning is a human decision step — designers make structure decisions before AI or tools automate visuals.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display both the high-fidelity mockup and a low-fidelity wireframe of the same web app with extras removed. Facilitate a brief discussion about which would be easier to change and why. Explain that designers sketch in low fidelity because it's faster and easier to adjust structure before visuals are added. This step keeps humans in control of key decisions before AI or developers build.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "65d3a569-181d-471d-b986-ed4f353185c6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "90cf0d6c-c83e-4545-b918-b53282cb3c48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a6a6fdb5-3188-49a2-a56b-727607ee3def",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You're the UX Designer deciding structure before AI or developers build. Your job is to make the layout clear and consistent so that anyone — or any AI tool — could understand how the app works. That's part of keeping humans in the loop.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a6a6fdb5-3188-49a2-a56b-727607ee3def",
        "lesson_activity.key": "90cf0d6c-c83e-4545-b918-b53282cb3c48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88f6af2b-8b6c-4e1f-a946-4369c14bfaf0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 3.\n- Level 1: Students create two wireframes each for a chosen a web app idea in Sketch Lab\n- Level 2: Students read the vocabulary definition for wireframe.\n- Level 3: Students add their own explanation or example of wireframe to the vocab free response.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students have chosen an app idea and understand its basic purpose. Look for consistent navigation and layout across pages. Prompt pairs to discuss why consistency is important for user experience. If groups struggle, remind them that AI can assist later with text or visuals — but humans design the structure first. Ask probing questions about how consistency helps users move through their app or why their layout make sense for their user persona.",
        "progression_name": "Wireframe an App Idea"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88f6af2b-8b6c-4e1f-a946-4369c14bfaf0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "90cf0d6c-c83e-4545-b918-b53282cb3c48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b73705dd-bf01-47d3-908f-bcd1761cafc0",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Wireframe",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize consistency across wireframes — this reinforces pattern recognition. If time allows, have students later prompt AI to generate a sample layout and compare it with their designs. This makes the human vs. AI interpretation explicit and previews upcoming AI lessons."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 1 to formatively assess student progress on lesson objectives. Check whether students can:\n- Create a low-fidelity wireframe for their web app's pages.\n- Map a user journey across pages in their wireframes."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b73705dd-bf01-47d3-908f-bcd1761cafc0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "90cf0d6c-c83e-4545-b918-b53282cb3c48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e3746d6-e631-4347-99b1-50a522a83775",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e3746d6-e631-4347-99b1-50a522a83775",
        "lesson_activity.key": "90cf0d6c-c83e-4545-b918-b53282cb3c48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "07ac0b31-c76f-4be0-90b1-e09a42a7d171",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now it's time to share your work and get feedback from your classmates. Remember — your goal isn't just to hear what they like, but to learn how clearly your design communicates structure and flow.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "07ac0b31-c76f-4be0-90b1-e09a42a7d171",
        "lesson_activity.key": "90cf0d6c-c83e-4545-b918-b53282cb3c48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f5f66a7b-a730-40b6-a6ff-45268ba878af",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have pairs share their user journey and wireframes with another pair. Students place sticky-note comments using the sentence starters. Encourage students to focus on clarity and usability, not visuals or polish.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen to the feedback being exchanged. Ensure students are providing specific and constructive feedback. Guide them if their feedback is too vague or not helpful.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use peer feedback to formatively assess student progress on lesson objectives. Check whether students can:\n- Evaluate and explain how clearly a wireframe communicates user flow.\n- Give and receive peer feedback on wireframe clarity."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f5f66a7b-a730-40b6-a6ff-45268ba878af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "90cf0d6c-c83e-4545-b918-b53282cb3c48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b1a0437-39cd-441a-a342-dc90d90dfeb4",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b1a0437-39cd-441a-a342-dc90d90dfeb4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "90cf0d6c-c83e-4545-b918-b53282cb3c48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b132caa0-bfc8-420f-acac-8f8934d83bce",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does wireframing make design work faster and clearer?*\n- *What design decisions must always stay human?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Encourage students to recognize that wireframing improves both clarity and efficiency by allowing designers to plan structure and fix problems early, before visuals or coding begin. Students should also understand that some decisions—like layout, usability, and user flow—must remain human choices because they require reasoning, empathy, and ethical awareness. This conversation reinforces the idea that humans lead the creative and structural stages of design, while AI can assist later with details and automation."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b132caa0-bfc8-420f-acac-8f8934d83bce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "90cf0d6c-c83e-4545-b918-b53282cb3c48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e142cf61-6cd0-4a9e-9527-af8f9b8e6e5f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students reflect on \"Glows\", \"Grows\", and \"Want to Knows\".\n- Glows: Which choices helped make your wireframe clear and easy to understand — for users or for AI later in the process?\n- Grows: What's one thing you could improve next time you wireframe?\n- Want-to-Knows: What questions do you still have about wireframing or the design process?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e142cf61-6cd0-4a9e-9527-af8f9b8e6e5f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "df30e8cf-8fdd-4c47-a82f-70661fa825a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e08e034d-dd97-41dc-a1da-f529762a7215",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 4 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e08e034d-dd97-41dc-a1da-f529762a7215",
        "lesson_activity.key": "df30e8cf-8fdd-4c47-a82f-70661fa825a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "36ef02d6-d45c-4b2a-a423-65016059e72d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize that combining structure (a map) with instructions (directions) leads to clearer results—introducing the idea that AI works best when given both a wireframe and a prompt.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which would you rather have if you were trying to find a new place?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should reason that having both written directions and a map makes it easier to understand and follow a plan because it provides structure and context. Listen for students noting that each on its own can be confusing or incomplete — setting up the idea that combining both gives clearer guidance."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "36ef02d6-d45c-4b2a-a423-65016059e72d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3326e8b-f489-4f3c-b7a6-0c2d4a057e87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "765c3150-80c0-4521-b5cc-6ee1b0611cfc",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Most people pick both, because the map gives structure and the directions explain the steps. The two work better together than alone. A map shows the big picture — where you are and where you're going — while the directions tell you how to get there. Keep that idea in mind, because today we'll see how the same thing applies when you work with AI.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "765c3150-80c0-4521-b5cc-6ee1b0611cfc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3326e8b-f489-4f3c-b7a6-0c2d4a057e87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "492f2a96-2821-4be6-b97b-3c68fa35c5b9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that the wireframe and written prompt help the AI understand what to build."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "492f2a96-2821-4be6-b97b-3c68fa35c5b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3326e8b-f489-4f3c-b7a6-0c2d4a057e87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "25043c3b-5b00-41ef-9481-27d1631e6766",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Just like maps and directions, your wireframe and prompt each do something different but complementary. The wireframe gives structure — it's the visual plan that shows where everything belongs on the page. The prompt adds meaning — it tells the AI what each part is for and what kind of content should go there. When you give AI both, it understands not just what to build, but why each piece matters.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "25043c3b-5b00-41ef-9481-27d1631e6766",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3326e8b-f489-4f3c-b7a6-0c2d4a057e87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "43806d1a-3f2a-495a-a6a3-d4286c8b0ed5",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What kinds of things might the AI understand or do better when it also has your wireframe as context?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should reason that giving AI visual context (like a wireframe) helps it make smarter, more aligned design choices — beyond just structure. Listen for ideas like:\n- matching the layout and proportions to their plan\n- choosing color schemes or images that fit the page's purpose\n- organizing content logically based on the design\n- generating code that's easier to adjust or style later\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "43806d1a-3f2a-495a-a6a3-d4286c8b0ed5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3326e8b-f489-4f3c-b7a6-0c2d4a057e87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0e3f1562-a9aa-496f-957c-122c5e4cbd36",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students generate their first AI-created layout from a wireframe using a structured prompt. They compare the AI output to their wireframe, identify matches and mismatches, and begin refining their process based on what they observe.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the From Wireframes to Layouts activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 1. Explain that they'll upload their wireframe from Lesson 10 and prompt the AI using the structured recipe.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Observe how students reason through the comparison. Ask guiding questions such as:\n- \"Where did the AI follow your wireframe well?\"\n- \"What doesn't match, and why do you think that happened?\"\n- \"What details might you add or change next time to get a closer match?\"",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Generate Using Wireframes",
        "progression_name": "Generate from a Wireframe",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If the AI's layout misses the mark, remind students they can clarify their prompt, add specific layout details, or generate smaller sections separately to get better results."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0e3f1562-a9aa-496f-957c-122c5e4cbd36",
        "lesson_activity.key": "24922c45-7cb9-46c5-a1f8-c788d473b331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8dbab7ca-5598-4a9a-8a22-12a826f64527",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8dbab7ca-5598-4a9a-8a22-12a826f64527",
        "lesson_activity.key": "24922c45-7cb9-46c5-a1f8-c788d473b331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c4af10d1-a81f-4d01-afd0-74b05bc08e79",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Did the AI omit any parts of your wireframe or miss user needs when generating your layout?*\n- *What might have caused that, and how did you adjust your prompt or code to fix it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that some omissions may come from unclear prompts, while others reflect the AI's own limitations. Listen for students identifying specific mismatches between their wireframe and the generated layout and considering whether those issues came from how they prompted or from how the AI interpreted the task. Students should discuss possible causes for mismatches, like vague wording, missing details, or AI misunderstanding structure. They should describe how they iterated to improve results. Listen for students showing accountability (clarifying or refining their prompt) while also recognizing when AI-generated outputs require human correction or more guidance."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c4af10d1-a81f-4d01-afd0-74b05bc08e79",
        "lesson_activity.key": "24922c45-7cb9-46c5-a1f8-c788d473b331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "43a8c7c1-2141-41cd-aaba-6bb4bb0fc9d0",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students refine their AI-generated layout by identifying targeted fixes, improving accessibility using the POUR checklist, and applying debugging strategies to resolve persistent issues. They iterate through multiple versions to move closer to a complete, functional design.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 2. Explain that this stage focuses on refining their layout and debugging issues that prevent it from matching their plan or being accessible. Students will upload their wireframe and use prompts to generate, refine, and debug their layout in Web Lab. They should use the Prompt Recipe Sheet, Refinement Checklist, and Debugging Checklist from their Unit Guide to help them iterate until the AI output matches their wireframe as closely as possible.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Encourage students to verbalize their reasoning:\n- \"What specific fix are you targeting in this version?\"\n- \"Which POUR principle did you apply, and why?\"\n- \"What debugging strategy are you trying, and how is it helping?\"\n\nListen for students describing how they test and refine their prompts systematically.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Generate Using Wireframes",
        "progression_name": "Refine, Debug, Iterate",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to view each version as an experiment that brings them closer to their intended design and improves their collaboration with AI."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "43a8c7c1-2141-41cd-aaba-6bb4bb0fc9d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "24922c45-7cb9-46c5-a1f8-c788d473b331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5673bf7c-1c61-4eaf-8616-570ef5eb5bdd",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How has your AI build workflow evolved now that you're translating wireframes into full layouts?*\n- *What's your responsibility when the AI's layout doesn't match your plan or your users' needs?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should describe how their workflow has become more intentional. Listen for students explaining that they now use wireframes as input to guide the AI, include clear goals or context in their prompts, and make targeted refinements to align structure with their plan. They should recognize that they're learning to communicate design intent more effectively to the AI. Students should identify that they are responsible for ensuring the final layout aligns with their design and user needs. Listen for students discussing how they revise prompts, debug or adjust code, and apply design principles to fix mismatches.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students complete the check for understanding on Level 3.",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student responses to gauge understanding of debugging and refinement. Consider displaying anonymous student responses on an interactive whiteboard to drive a class debrief."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5673bf7c-1c61-4eaf-8616-570ef5eb5bdd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "24922c45-7cb9-46c5-a1f8-c788d473b331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "602dac65-df60-4505-9308-9179ab11338e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "602dac65-df60-4505-9308-9179ab11338e",
